speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, under the new clause, the corporation may acquire undertakings "either by lease or by purchase," but it also mentions that the C.B.C. may "acquire, hold and dispose of shares of the capital stock." This does not mean the purchase of a company, but it may very well mean holding 40, 50, 60 per cent of the shares of a company, whereas under the former act, the C.B.C. could buy a existing station. Now we are told that the corporation can become a co-owner. In addition, the former act mentioned "broadcasting stations," whereas the new legislation does not mention anything at all. Provision is merely made for the corporation to:
(m) ... acquire, hold and dispose of shares of the capital stock of any company or corporation authorized to carry on any business that is incidental or conducive to the attainment of the objects of the Corporation;
The new legislation goes much further than the former act, because it is not restricted to "broadcasting stations", but includes even newspapers and film companies. And, as I mentioned earlier, the corporation could become the owner of a hockey or baseball team as happened in the United States.
